              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            (originally:
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+logic"} intuitionistic logic
            %em BHK interpretation
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+connectives"} logical connectives
            (due to
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Kolmogorov32"} Kolmogorov (1932, p. 59)
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Troelstra69"} Troelstra (1969, §2)
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Attribution"} below
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proposition"} proposition
            is regarded as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/true"} true
            only if it is possible to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} construct
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof"} proof
            of its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/assertion"} assertion
          %p
            For instance, to assert a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+conjunction"} logical conjunction
            (“and”) or a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+quantification"} universal quantification
            (“for all”) is taken to mean to provide a proof for all the instances. Dually, but more notably, to assert a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+disjunction"} logical disjunction
            (“or”) or an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/existential+quantification"} existential quantification
            (“exists”) is taken to mean to prove one of the instances, so that there is no intuitionistic existence statement without construction of an example (the “disjunction property”, see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "intuitionistic+logic#DisjunctionProperty"} there
          %p
            This constructive interpretation of logical truth is the crux of the rejection of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+excluded+middle"} principle of excluded middle
            in
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionism"} intuitionism
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo ∨
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ¬
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \vee (\not P)
            (which may superficially/classically seem tautologous) one must prove
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            or one must prove
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ¬
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \not P
            — but neither proof may be known (e.g. if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            \=
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+hypothesis"} Riemann hypothesis
          %p
            In short this means that a proposition is regarded a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/true"} true
            if there is an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algorithm"} algorithm
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computable+function"} computable function
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof"} proof
            whence one also speaks of the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/realizability"} realizability
                interpretation
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions+as+types"} propositions as types
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proofs+as+programs"} proofs as programs
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Curry-Howard+correspondence"} Curry-Howard correspondence
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
          %p
            Indeed, the fully formal version of the BHK interpretation may be understood as being the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inference+rules"} inference rules
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term+introduction+rules"} term introduction rules
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+type+theory"} intuitionistic type theory
            (as amplified in
            %a{:href => "#Girard89"} Girard (1989, §2)
            and
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#MartinLöf96"} Martin-Löf (1996, Lec 3)
          %h2#in_mathematical_foundations In mathematical foundations
          %h3#dependent_type_theory  Dependent type theory
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsingletons"} subsingletons
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            in which given
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x:A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y:A
            one can construct an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identification"} identification
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Id
                      %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p(x, y):\mathrm{Id}_A(x, y)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            are families of subsingletons
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(x)
            indexed by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x:A
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BHK+interpretation"} BHK interpretation
            of predicate logic is defined using basic type theoretic operations: Given two propositions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            or
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            is given by an element of the binary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sum+type"} sum type
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo +
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P + Q
          %p
            and the pure existential quantifier of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicate"} predicate
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(x)
            on a type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is given by an element of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+sum+type"} dependent sum type
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_{x:A} P(x)
          %p Also,
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                false is given by the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+type"} empty type
            %li
              %p
                true is given by any
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contractible+type"} contractible type
            %li
              %p
                implication is given by the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+type"} function type
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mo →
                      %mi Q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \to Q
            %li
              %p
                conjunction is given by the binary
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+type"} product type
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mo ×
                      %mi Q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \times Q
            %li
              %p
                and the universal quantification is given by the indexed
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+product"} Cartesian product
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo :
                          %mi A
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prod_{x:A} P(x)
          %h3#set_theory Set theory
          %p
            The BHK interpretation of logic is also possible to express in set theory; in particular, in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            of the resulting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sets"} category of sets
            that arises from the set theory.
          %p
            Propositions in the internal logic are given by the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsingletons"} subsingletons
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            in which for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x = y
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singleton"} singleton
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{*\}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            can be turned into an subsingleton via the subset
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi p
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo =
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{p \in \{*\} \vert (p = *) \wedge P\}
          %p
            This means that one can use the set-theoretic operations to define the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BHK+interpretation"} BHK interpretation
            of predicate logic: Given two propositions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            or
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            is given by an element of the binary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+union"} disjoint union
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi p
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo =
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ⊎
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi p
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo =
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi Q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{p \in \{*\} \vert (p = *) \wedge P\} \uplus \{p \in \{*\} \vert (p = *) \wedge Q\}
          %p
            and the pure existential quantifier of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicate"} predicate
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(x)
            on a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is given by an element of the indexed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+union"} disjoint union
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨄
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi p
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo =
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \biguplus_{x \in A} \{p \in \{*\} \vert (p = *) \wedge P(x)\}
          %p Also,
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                false is given by the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+set"} empty set
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi p
                      %mo ∈
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi p
                      %mo =
                      %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∧
                      %mo ⊥
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{p \in \{*\} \vert (p = *) \wedge \bot\}
            %li
              %p
                true is given by any
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singleton"} singleton
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi p
                      %mo ∈
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi p
                      %mo =
                      %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∧
                      %mo ⊤
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{p \in \{*\} \vert (p = *) \wedge \top\}
            %li
              %p
                implication is given by the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+set"} function set
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                          %mi p
                          %mo ∈
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                          %mo *
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi p
                          %mo =
                          %mo *
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi Q
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                          %mi p
                          %mo ∈
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                          %mo *
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi p
                          %mo =
                          %mo *
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi P
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\{p \in \{*\} \vert (p = *) \wedge Q\}}^{\{p \in \{*\} \vert (p = *) \wedge P\} }
            %li
              %p
                conjunction is given by the binary
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+product"} Cartesian product
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi p
                      %mo ∈
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi p
                      %mo =
                      %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo ×
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi p
                      %mo ∈
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi p
                      %mo =
                      %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi Q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{p \in \{*\} \vert (p = *) \wedge P\} \times \{p \in \{*\} \vert (p = *) \wedge Q\}
            %li
              %p
                and the universal quantification is given by the indexed
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+product"} Cartesian product
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi p
                      %mo ∈
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi p
                      %mo =
                      %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prod_{x \in A} \{p \in \{*\} \vert (p = *) \wedge P(x)\}
          %p Using this one can translate any theorems and proofs of set-level mathematics done in dependent type theory using the BHK interpretation over to set theory.
          %h2#HistoricalVersions Historical versions
          %p
            The BHK interpretation evolved from a rather informal statement in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Kolmogorov32"} Kolmogorov (1932, p. 59)
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Troelstra69"} Troelstra (1969, p. 5)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inference+rules"} inference rules
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+type+theory"} intuitionistic type theory
            due to
            %a{:href => "Martin-Löf+dependent+type+theory#MartinLof75"} Martin-Löf 75
            (gentle exposition in
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#MartinLöf96"} Martin-Löf (1996, Lec 3)
          %p
            From
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#Kolmogorov32"} Kolmogorov (1932, p. 59)
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/KolmogorovIntroducingBHK.jpg", :width => "540px"}/
          %p
            From
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#Heyting56"} Heyting (1956, p. 97)
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/HeytingIntroducingBHK.jpg", :width => "500px"}/
          %p
            From
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#Troelstra69"} Troelstra (1969, p. 5)
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/Troelstra-IntroducingBHKInterp.jpg", :width => "660px"}/
          %p
            From
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#Troelstra77"} Troelstra (1977, p. 977)
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/Troelstra-AttributingBHK.jpg", :width => "500px"}/
          %p#FromTroelstraVanDalen88
            From
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#TroelstraVanDalen88"} Troelstra &amp; van Dalen (1988)
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/Troelstra-VanDalen-BHKInterpretation.jpg", :width => "500px"}/
          %p#FromBridges
            From
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#Bridges99"} Bridges (1999), p. 96
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/Bridges-IntuitInterpOfConnectives.jpg", :width => "600px"}/
          %p
            The analogous discussion for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inference+rules"} inference rules
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+type+theory"} intuitionistic type theory
            is then given in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#MartinLof75"} Martin-Löf (1975)
            %a{:href => "#Girard89"} Girard (1989, §2)
            and with more emphasis in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#MartinLöf96"} Martin-Löf (1996, Lec 3)
          %h2#Attribution Attribution
          %p
            The idea of the interpretation is clearly expressed in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Kolmogorov32"} Kolmogorov (1932, p. 59)
            = succeed "," do
              %em propositions
            %em Aufgaben
            (Deutsch for “tasks”, but here in the sense used in math classes where it means “exercises” or “mathematical problems”) in order to amplify the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive
            aspects (he expands it out to
            = succeed "," do
              %em Konstruktionsaufgabe
          %p
            While
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Arend+Heyting"} Arend Heyting
            may have been speaking of the idea of the “proof interpretation” of propositions since the 1930s, the first recognizable statement of what later was called the BHK interpretation is given in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Heyting56"} Heyting (1956, §7.1.1)
          %p
            The statement of the interpretation close to its modern formulation (but clearly of the same conceptual content as previously expressed in
            %a{:href => "#Kolmogorov32"} Kolmogorov (1932, p. 59)
            and
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Heyting56"} Heyting (1956, §7.1.1)
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Troelstra69"} Troelstra (1969, §2)
          %p
            Later,
            %a{:href => "#Troelstra77"} Troelstra (1977, §2)
            credits the idea to “Brouwer-Heyting-
            %em Kreisel
            (BHK)”, not mentioning Kolmogorov — indeed
            %a{:href => "#Kreisel65"} Kreisel (1965)
            is the only reference that
            %a{:href => "#Troelstra69"} Troelstra (1969, §2)
            makes explicit use of. (While this is the origin of the term “BHK interpretation”, the adjustment/correction of its expanded version by exchanging “
            %em> K
            reisel” for “
            %em> K
            olmogorov” seems to be due to
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a{:href => "#Troelstra90"} Troelstra (1990, §2.1)
          %p
            Still later,
            %a{:href => "#Girard89"} Girard (1989, §1.2.2)
            presents the same idea as “Heyting’s semantics” (not mentioning anyone else, nor actually citing Heyting).
          %p
            More discussion of this history is in
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/intuitionistic-logic-development/"} SEP: “The Development of Intuitionistic Logic”
          %p
            Notice that Brouwer never explicitly formulated any interpretation of the kind that
            %a{:href => "#Troelstra77"} Troelstra (1977, §2)
            attributes to him, and in fact remained against all formalism his entire life. (His role in this history is to provide motivation and inspiration for Heyting and Kolmogorov.) Moreover,
            %a{:href => "#EscardoXu15"} Escardo &amp; Xu (2015)
            have shown that Brouwer’s famous intuitionistic theorem “all functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mi ℕ
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}^{\mathbb{N}} \to \mathbb{N}
            are continuous” is actually inconsistent under a literal version of this interpretation (i.e. without including
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+truncation"} propositional truncation
          %p Thus, perhaps it should be called the “Heyting-Kolmogorov” interpretation.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/realizability+topos"} realizability topos
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computational+trinitarianism"} computational trinitarianism
          %h2#references References
          %p
            Original articles on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionism"} intuitionism
            and, eventually, on
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+logic"} intuitionistic logic
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Arend+Heyting"}> Arend Heyting
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Die intuitionistische Grundlegung der Mathematik
                %strong 2
                (1931) 106-115 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://www.jstor.org/stable/20011630"}> jsotr:20011630
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.psiquadrat.de/downloads/heyting1931.pdf"} pdf
            %li#Kolmogorov32
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrey+Kolmogorov"}> Andrey Kolmogorov
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Zur Deutung der intuitionistischen Logik
                %strong 35
                (1932) 58-65 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF01186549"}> doi:10.1007/BF01186549
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hans+Freudenthal"}> Hans Freudenthal
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Zur intuitionistischen Deutung logischer Formeln
                %strong 4
                (1937) 112-116 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/?id=CM_1937__4__112_0"}> numdam:CM_1937__4__112_0
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Arend+Heyting"}> Arend Heyting
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Bemerkungen zu dem Aufsatz von Herrn Freudenthal “Zur intuitionistischen Deutung logischer Formeln”
                %strong 4
                (1937) 117-118 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/?id=CM_1937__4__117_0"}> doi:CM_1937__4__117_0
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L.+E.+J.+Brouwer"}> L. E. J. Brouwer
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Points and Spaces
                %strong 6
                (1954) 1-17 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.4153/CJM-1954-001-9"}> doi:10.4153/CJM-1954-001-9
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p Early monographs:
          %ul
            %li#Heyting56
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Arend+Heyting"}> Arend Heyting
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Intuitionism: An introduction
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => ""} ISBN:978-0720422399
            %li#Kreisel65
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Kreisel"}> Georg Kreisel
                , Section 2 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Mathematical Logic
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Lectures on Modern Mathematics III
            %li#TroelstraVanDalen88
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anne+Sjerp+Troelstra"}> Anne Sjerp Troelstra
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirk+van+Dalen"} Dirk van Dalen
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Constructivism in Mathematics – An introduction
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 121
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.elsevier.com/books/constructivism-in-mathematics-vol-1/troelstra/978-0-444-70266-1"} ISBN:9780444702661
          %p Early historical review:
          %ul
            %li#Troelstra90
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anne+Sjerp+Troelstra"}> Anne Sjerp Troelstra
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em On the Early History of Intuitionistic Logic
              = succeed "," do
                %em Mathematical Logic
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4613-0609-2_1"} doi:10.1007/978-1-4613-0609-2_1
          %p Recognizable formulation of the so-called “BHK interretation” first appears in:
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#Kolmogorov32"} Kolmogorov (1932, p. 59)
          %p
            (who however speaks not of propositions but of
            = succeed "," do
              %em Aufgaben
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#Heyting56"} Heyting (1956, §7.1.1)
          %p (who is maybe the first to speak of the “meaning of logical connectives”) and then
          %ul
            %li#Troelstra69
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anne+Sjerp+Troelstra"}> Anne Sjerp Troelstra
              , §2 of:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Principles of Intuitionism
              %strong 95
              Springer Heidelberg (1969) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/BFb0080643"}> doi:10.1007/BFb0080643
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p (where it is presented as if the author’s invention) and then recalled in
          %ul
            %li#Troelstra77
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anne+Sjerp+Troelstra"}> Anne Sjerp Troelstra
              , §2 of:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Aspects of Constructive Mathematics
              %strong 90
              973-1052 (1977) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/S0049-237X(08)71127-3"}> doi:10.1016/S0049-237X(08)71127-3
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            (where the same is now called the “Brouwer-Heyting-
            %em Kreisel (BHK)
            explanation” — not mentioning Kolmogorov).
          %p
            and later recalled in the context of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+analysis"} constructive analysis
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Douglas+Bridges"}> Douglas Bridges
              , p. 96 of:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Constructive mathematics: a foundation for computable analysis
              %strong 219
              1–2 (1999) 95-109 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/S0304-3975(98)00285-0"}> doi:10.1016/S0304-3975(98)00285-0
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            This lead to the formulation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+type+theory"} intuitionistic type theory
            in
          %ul
            %li#MartinLof75
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Per+Martin-L%C3%B6f"}> Per Martin-Löf
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em An intuitionistic theory of types: predicative part
              = succeed "," do
                %em Logic Colloquium ‘73, Proceedings of the Logic Colloquium
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 80
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/S0049-237X(08)71945-1"} doi:10.1016/S0049-237X(08)71945-1
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/viewdoc/summary?doi=10.1.1.131.926"} CiteSeer
          %p reviewed in
          %ul
            %li#Girard89
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Yves+Girard"} Jean-Yves Girard
              (translated and with appendices by
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Taylor"} Paul Taylor
              and
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yves+Lafont"} Yves Lafont
              = succeed "," do
                %em Proofs and Types
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => ""} ISBN:978-0-521-37181-0
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.paultaylor.eu/stable/Proofs+Types.html"} webpage
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.paultaylor.eu/stable/prot.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            (where it is called
            = succeed ")" do
              %em Heyting semantics
          %ul
            %li{:id => "MartinLöf96"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Per+Martin-L%C3%B6f"}> Per Martin-Löf
              , Lecture 3 of:
              = succeed "," do
                %em On the Meanings of the Logical Constants and the Justifications of the Logical Laws
              %strong 1
              1 (1996) 11-60 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://docenti.lett.unisi.it/files/4/1/1/6/martinlof4.pdf"}> pdf
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/MartinLofOnTheMeaning96.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Wikipedia,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BHK_interpretation"} BHK interpretation
            %li
              %p
                Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/intuitionistic-logic-development/#ProoInte"} The Development of Intuitionistic Logic
            %li
              %p
                Wouter Pieter Stekelenburg,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Realizability Categories
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1301.2134"} arXiv:1301.2134
            %li#EscardoXu15
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin+Escardo"}> Martin Escardo
                , Chuangjie Xu,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The inconsistency of a Brouwerian continuity principle with the Curry–Howard interpretation
                %em 38
                (2015) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.TLCA.2015.153"}> doi:10.4230/LIPIcs.TLCA.2015.153
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.cs.bham.ac.uk/%7Emhe/papers/escardo-xu-inconsistency-continuity.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                E. G. F. Díez,
                %em Five observations concerning the intended meaning of the intuitionistic logical constants
                , J. Phil. Logic
                %strong 29
                no. 4 (2000) pp.409–424 . (
                %a{:href => "https://webs.um.es/picazo/miwiki/lib/exe/fetch.php?id=inicio&cache=cache&media=five_observations_2000_jour_of_phil_logic_29_4_pp409_424.pdf"}> preprint
                )
          %p
            Links to many papers on realizability and related topics may be found
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://www.staff.science.uu.nl/~ooste110/realizability.html"} here
          %p For a comment see also
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Harper"}> Robert Harper
              ,
              %em Extensionality, Intensionality, and Brouwer’s Dictum
              (
              %a{:href => "http://existentialtype.wordpress.com/2012/08/11/extensionality-intensionality-and-brouwers-dictum/"}> web
              )
          .property
